% Implement the source-adapter contract that lists rows from one Socrata resource endpoint as provenance-stamped source pages.

<include>prompts/context/sitevelocity-preamble.prompt</include>

<responsibility>
List rows from one declared Socrata resource endpoint as provenance-stamped source pages, including batched lookups of a declared key set.
</responsibility>

<non_responsibilities>
MUST NOT canonicalize, normalize, enrich, qualify, rank, or score a retrieved row.
MUST NOT deduplicate, merge, or reconcile rows within a page, across pages, or across batches.
MUST NOT read any endpoint other than the one carried by the declared source descriptor.
MUST NOT open its own network connection; every retrieval MUST pass through the injected source transport.
MUST NOT read a clock other than the one supplied through its declared options, which fall back to the system clock only when the caller declares none.
MUST NOT infer an undeclared field mapping, an undeclared predicate, or an undeclared query parameter.
MUST NOT surface provider SDK types, HTTP client types, or transport types in its declared outputs.
</non_responsibilities>

<vocabulary>
declared: named explicitly by the caller in the module inputs, never inferred from retrieved row content
source transport: an injected function that maps one URL to one already-parsed response body, so the module is exercised offline and deterministically
injected clock: an injected function that supplies the retrieval instant, the module's only declared external dependency
source page: one retrieval from one query URL carrying the source descriptor, that query URL, the retrieval timestamp, the page number, the enveloped rows in endpoint order, and a continuation flag
short page: a retrieved row array whose length is smaller than the requested `$limit`
page cap: the declared maximum number of pages one query or one batch may retrieve, which bounds paging when no short page arrives
bare field name: a string matching one ASCII letter or underscore followed by ASCII letters, digits, or underscores
declared predicate: one member of the closed predicate set the module accepts — field equals literal, field within a key set, or field is not null
key set: an ordered list of declared string keys the caller asks to look up against one declared key field
batch: one contiguous slice of the key set whose length is at most the declared batch size
returned key: a requested key for which at least one retrieved row carries a value at the declared key field whose string form is character-for-character equal to that requested key, whether or not the shared source-record envelope accepted that row
not-returned key: a requested key that is not a returned key; it records only that the endpoint returned no such row, and it never records that no such record exists
row refusal: a returned entry naming the query URL, page number, and row position of a row the shared source-record envelope declined, retained so no row disappears without a trace
declared failure: a returned outcome object carrying a stable failure code and a non-sensitive explanation, which the caller can tell apart from every retrieved page
credential: URL userinfo, or a query parameter whose name denotes a token, key, secret, password, signature, or authorization
</vocabulary>

<contract_rules>
R1 (MUST): Obtain every row through the injected source transport, from URLs built only from the declared source descriptor endpoint plus declared query parameters.
R2 (MUST): Compose a query URL from the declared endpoint using only the Socrata parameters `$select`, `$where`, `$order`, `$limit`, and `$offset`, emitted in that fixed order.
R3 (MUST): Request the first page at the declared `$offset` and each later page at the previous offset increased by the declared `$limit`.
R4 (MUST): Set a page continuation flag to false on a short page and stop paging there; set it to true on any other page.
R5 (MUST): Build `$where` only from declared predicates over bare field names; a field name that is not a bare field name is a declared failure and no transport call is made.
R6 (MUST NOT): let any character inside a declared string key end, extend, or add a predicate term; the built `$where` MUST carry exactly one quoted literal per declared key.
R7 (MUST): Preserve endpoint row order inside each page, and preserve retrieval order across the returned pages and batches.
R8 (MUST): Envelope every retrieved row through the shared source-record envelope using the declared identifier fields, and record a row refusal for every row the envelope declines.
R9 (MUST): Preserve the declared key set character-for-character in declared order, including repeated keys, and query it in contiguous batches of the declared batch size.
R10 (MUST): Account for every requested key exactly once — one accounting entry per occurrence in the declared key set, in declared order — as either a returned key or a not-returned key.
R11 (MUST): Represent a not-returned key as an outcome whose existence is unknown, which the caller can tell apart from an outcome carrying zero rows and from an assertion that no such record exists.
R12 (MUST): Continue paging inside a batch until a short page or the page cap, so a key carrying more rows than one page is still accounted for.
R13 (MUST): Emit an identical result for identical declared inputs, an identical ordered sequence of transport responses, and identical injected clock readings.
R14 (MUST): Report a response body that is not a JSON array as a declared failure rather than as a page carrying zero rows.
R15 (MUST): Report a rejected transport call as a declared failure naming the query URL that was attempted.
R16 (MUST): Check the source descriptor and the query inputs before the first transport call, and refuse a descriptor endpoint that carries a query string or a credential.
R17 (MUST NOT): place a credential in any built query URL, page, accounting entry, row refusal, or declared failure.
R18 (MUST NOT): produce rows, record identifiers, key values, or retrieval timestamps that the transport responses and the injected clock did not supply; a continuation flag MUST be derived from the retrieved row count against the requested `$limit` and from nothing else.
R19 (MUST NOT): let text inside a retrieved row change paging, predicates, enveloping, or the built query URL; the only use of row text is reading the declared key field to account for requested keys.
R20 (MUST NOT): sort, filter, deduplicate, merge, or reconcile rows within a page, across pages, or across batches.
R21 (MUST): Stop paging at the page cap and report that the cap ended retrieval, which the caller can tell apart from a retrieval that ended at a short page.
</contract_rules>

<pdd-interface>
This module imports its shared vocabulary from the Segment 2 source-record envelope module and re-declares none of it: `JsonValue`, `SourceDescriptor`, `SourceRecord`, `SourcePage`, `SourceTransport`, `EnvelopeRefusal`, `toSourceRecord`, and `toSourcePage`.

`SocrataRow` is an object whose values are `JsonValue`.

`SourceClock` is a function taking no argument and returning a point in time. `SocrataAdapterOptions` carries the injected `transport` and an optional `now` clock; the clock is the module's only declared external dependency.

`SocrataPredicate` is exactly one of:
- `{ kind: "equals", field: string, value: string }`
- `{ kind: "keyIn", field: string, values: string[] }`
- `{ kind: "isNotNull", field: string }`

`SocrataListQuery` carries `identifierFields`, `limit`, and optional `offset`, `maxPages`, `select`, `order`, and `where` predicates. `maxPages` is the page cap.

`SocrataKeySetQuery` carries `identifierFields`, `keyField`, `keys`, `batchSize`, `limit`, and optional `maxPagesPerBatch`, `select`, `order`, and `additionalPredicates`. `maxPagesPerBatch` is the per-batch page cap.

A declared query input of the wrong shape — a non-array where a list is declared, a non-integer where a count is declared — is a declared failure, never a thrown error.

`SocrataFailure` carries a stable `code` drawn from a closed set covering a malformed descriptor, malformed query inputs, a malformed field name, a non-array response body, and a rejected transport call; the attempted `queryUrl` or null; and a non-sensitive `explanation`. No failure, refusal, page, or accounting entry carries a credential.

`SocrataRowRefusal` carries `queryUrl`, `batchNumber` or null, `pageNumber`, `rowIndex`, and the `EnvelopeRefusal` list the shared envelope produced. A refused row is still counted as a returned key, because the endpoint returned it.

`SocrataKeyAccount` is exactly one of:
- `{ key, status: "returned", rowCount, batchNumbers }`
- `{ key, status: "not_returned", existence: "unknown", explanation }`

`SocrataBatchResult` carries `batchNumber`, the batch's `keys` in declared order, and the batch's `SourcePage` list in retrieval order.

`listSocrataRows(descriptor, query, options)` resolves to either `{ status: "listed", pages, rowRefusals, stoppedAtPageLimit }` or `{ status: "failed", failure }`.

`lookupSocrataRowsByKey(descriptor, keySetQuery, options)` resolves to either `{ status: "looked_up", batches, accounting, rowRefusals, stoppedAtPageLimit }` or `{ status: "failed", failure }`.

`stoppedAtPageLimit` is true only when the page cap ended retrieval while the last retrieved page still carried a true continuation flag (R21).

Neither function throws for a declared failure; both return a failure outcome instead.
</pdd-interface>

<acceptance_criteria>
Every rule `R1` through `R21` and inherited rule `P1` through `P8` is mapped to at least one executable behavioral test before generation is accepted.
Tests inject a stub source transport and a fixed clock and reach no network.
</acceptance_criteria>
